course: dictionary
lesson: 13657
title: Inflectional endings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An inflection is a small change to the end of a word that shows grammar, such as who does an action or when it happens. «She walks to school every day.» The -s shows that one person walks, and it happens now.
explain: Inflectional endings are -s, -'s, -ed, -ing, -en, -er and -est. They change the grammar of a word, but not its basic meaning or its word class. «Walk», «walked» and «walking» are all still verbs about walking.
explain: Look for the ending first, then ask what it tells you. «The tallest tree» has -est, which shows the tree is the most tall of all. The word is still an adjective, so it describes a noun.
fact: Inflectional means changing the form of a word to show grammar.
fact: An inflectional ending is added to the end of a word, like «-ed» in «jumped».
fact: Inflectional endings change grammar, but the basic meaning and word class stay the same: «tall», «taller» and «tallest» are all adjectives.
fact: The plural -s, as in «cats», and the possessive -'s, as in «Sam's bag», are both inflectional endings.
fact: An inflectional language changes its word endings a lot to show grammar. English uses fewer endings than many languages, such as Latin.
exercise: Which ending is inflectional in «jumping»? | -ful | -ly | -ness | *-ing
exercise: «Sam's bag is red.» What does the -'s show? | Sam is a verb | There are many Sams | *The bag belongs to Sam | The bag is sad
exercise: Which pair shows the same word with an inflectional change? | *tall and taller | teacher and teach | happy and unhappy | kind and kindness
test: «Yesterday Mia painted the fence.» Which form shows the action happened in the past? | I paint | I paints | I painting | *I painted
test: Which ending turns «bright» into «brightest»? | -ful | -ly | *-est | -ness
exam: Which word shows more than one cat? | catful | catness | *cats | cat
